\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Schematic overview of the mixing problem using a bivariate mixture of normal distributions as an example. a) density functions of two synthetic source populations, and b) a 1/2-mixture of these sources (i.e., $W_1=1/3$ and $W_2=2/3$). c) and d) are the corresponding cumulative distributions. e) and f) are empirical cumulative distribution functions of random samples of 100 values from each of the three populations. $X_{1,i}$, $X_{2,i}$ and $Y_{i}$ are the cumulative probabilities under the source and sink distributions for the $i$\textsuperscript  {th} measurement. These values are used to populate Equation~\ref {eq:unmix}.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:algorithm}{{1}{3}{Schematic overview of the mixing problem using a bivariate mixture of normal distributions as an example. a) density functions of two synthetic source populations, and b) a 1/2-mixture of these sources (i.e., $W_1=1/3$ and $W_2=2/3$). c) and d) are the corresponding cumulative distributions. e) and f) are empirical cumulative distribution functions of random samples of 100 values from each of the three populations. $X_{1,i}$, $X_{2,i}$ and $Y_{i}$ are the cumulative probabilities under the source and sink distributions for the $i$\textsuperscript {th} measurement. These values are used to populate Equation~\ref {eq:unmix}}{figure.1}{}}
